{"kind":"request","text":"Review after E3: the method `explore`, one result value, and a field for the end of a record.\n\nFiled 2026-11-14 14:53 EST. Sections 13 and 16 of `notes/2026-11-14-act-environment.md` schedule a review after step E3 for the method `explore` on `ScopeApi`. No request held that review. Hugh agreed today to add items 2 and 3.\n\n## When\n\nThe review starts when E3 (`17e075b3`) lands on main. E2 (`a102d847`) does not wait for the review. The answer of the MCP tool `explore` is outside the kernel and can change after E2.\n\n## Items\n\n1. The method `explore` on `ScopeApi`, which section 16 holds. The review rules that the method enters, stays held, or is not taken. The ruling follows `docs/kernel.md` section 7.\n\n2. One result value. Hugh proposed on 14 November that each result of an act has one structure: an ambiguous value that holds the verdict and each end that is still possible. The candidate folds `Exploration`, `Judged`, the request status and `Why` into one type. That type holds the instance's verdict, the scope's latest answer, and the ends that the evidence still permits. One key has three ends: this version records, another version records, or the record ends with no entry. `explore` and `submit` stay two calls, because Explore gives no authority.\n\n   The planner's reading of 14 November: a page author writes almost the same code under the candidate, so the fold needs evidence. The fold is worth its cost if pages match the verdict in one place and the status in another place with repeated code. The pinboard and the map move after E5 (section 13), so the review after E3 has only E3's own code, the suite and the reservation scenario of step R. If that evidence does not decide the item, the review holds the fold until the pinboard and the map move. While the fold is open, the method of item 1 stays held, because an answer shape in `packages/contract` is closed.\n\n3. A field that says whether a request can still record. The client note (D12, `notes/2026-11-13-scope-client.md`) says in section 6.2 that `refused` and `withdrawn` are statements about one head and one clock. `requestStatus` at `cc47856622` (`packages/client/src/request-status.ts`, branch `builder/c3-requests-20261113`) answers `refused` for a request with a copy in flight that the replica's judge refuses at its head, and for a request whose record ended. No member of `RequestStatus` tells those two apart. An agent that reads `refused` as final can make a new step, and a late copy of the first step can then record. The review rules on one field, in the request view and in the answer of the MCP tool `settle`.\n\n## Not on the agenda\n\nA choice between acts with backtracking (`amb` as control). Section 8.1 rules that a failed step selects no other act, and section 7.2 requires a gesture for each version. That reading is the planner's. Hugh did not rule on it.\n\n## Filing rule\n\nThis request adds no scope kind, no platform definition name, no capability name or form, no tool family and no record model. Item 1 is the kernel part that section 16 already holds.","body":{"conditions":"A ruling on each of the three items lands on main through review, in notes/2026-11-14-act-environment.md or in a dated note that it links. Hugh decides each item. The ruling on item 2 names the code that it read. No source for the method explore on ScopeApi starts before the rulings on items 1 and 2.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}